  "question": {
    "text": "Let $P(x, y, z)$ be a point in the first octant, whose projection in the $x y$-plane is the point $Q$. Let $O P=\\gamma$; the angle between $O Q$ and the positive $x$-axis be $\\theta$; and the angle between $O P$ and the positive $z$-axis be $\\phi$, where $O$ is the origin. Then the distance of $P$ from the $x$-axis is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$\\gamma \\sqrt{1-\\sin ^2 \\phi \\cos ^2 \\theta}$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\gamma \\sqrt{1-\\sin ^2 \\theta \\cos ^2 \\phi}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\gamma \\sqrt{1+\\cos ^2 \\phi \\sin ^2 \\theta}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\gamma \\sqrt{1+\\cos ^2 \\theta \\sin ^2 \\phi}$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned} & \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{x}, \\mathrm{y}, \\mathrm{z}), \\mathrm{Q}(\\mathrm{x}, \\mathrm{y}, \\mathrm{O}) ; \\mathrm{x}^2+\\mathrm{y}^2+\\mathrm{z}^2=\\gamma^2 \\\\ & \\overline{\\mathrm{OQ}}=\\mathrm{x} \\hat{\\mathrm{i}}+\\mathrm{y} \\hat{\\mathrm{j}} \\\\ & \\cos \\theta=\\frac{\\mathrm{x}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{x}^2+\\mathrm{y}^2}} \\\\ & \\cos \\phi=\\frac{\\mathrm{z}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{x}^2+\\mathrm{y}^2+\\mathrm{z}^2}} \\\\ & \\Rightarrow \\sin ^2 \\phi=\\frac{\\mathrm{x}^2+\\mathrm{y}^2}{\\mathrm{x}^2+\\mathrm{y}^2+\\mathrm{z}^2}\\end{aligned}$\ndistance of $\\mathrm{P}$ from $\\mathrm{x}$-axis $\\sqrt{\\mathrm{y}^2+\\mathrm{z}^2}$\n$\\begin{aligned} & \\Rightarrow \\sqrt{\\gamma^2-x^2} \\Rightarrow \\gamma \\sqrt{1-\\frac{x^2}{\\gamma^2}} \\\\ & =\\gamma \\sqrt{1-\\cos ^2 \\theta \\sin ^2 \\phi}\\end{aligned}$"
  }
}